[0m[0m
Three details drove the design:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The C's result depends on [3mrelative heap addresses[23m, not just values — [38;5;10mcompute_hash[0m scores [38;5;10mmb1->data < mb2->data[0m and [38;5;10mmb1 < mb2[0m, and [38;5;10mbetagamma[0m tests [38;5;10mmem1->data != mem2->data[0m and [38;5;10m> NULL[0m. Reproducing that required calling libc [38;5;10mmalloc[0m/[38;5;10mcalloc[0m/[38;5;10mfree[0m directly rather than Rust's allocator, so allocation ordering matches. In practice this yields [38;5;10mhash = 110[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10msize_t block_size = (param1 % 10) + 5[0m is computed in [38;5;10mint[0m, so a negative [38;5;10mparam1 % 10[0m of -6 or lower sign-extends into a huge [38;5;10msize_t[0m, [38;5;10mcalloc[0m fails, and the function returns -1. That's why inputs like [38;5;10mparam1 = -7[0m, [38;5;10m-6[0m, [38;5;10m-9[0m, and [38;5;10mINT_MIN[0m return -1 while [38;5;10m-5[0m returns a normal result. I preserved this rather than treating it as a bug, per your instruction.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Integer overflow is expressed with explicit [38;5;10mwrapping_*[0m operations so release-mode Rust matches gcc's two's-complement wraparound instead of panicking in debug builds — relevant since [38;5;10mparam1 = INT_MAX[0m legitimately overflows and yields [38;5;10m-2147483172[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The dead [38;5;10mstrcpy(temp_name, current->name)[0m in the loop is retained for fidelity, as is [38;5;10mcreate_block[0m's unbounded [38;5;10mstrcpy[0m into the 32-byte [38;5;10mname[0m field. I did not add bounds checks there — doing so would change behavior on over-long names. Note this means [38;5;10mcreate_block[0m remains overflowable by a caller passing a name of 32+ bytes, exactly as in the original; if you ever want that hardened, it's a deliberate behavioral change to make separately. Nothing in [38;5;10mc_src/[0m was modified (checksums unchanged).[0m[0m
